:"Triangle sums and a linear pair","methodDirectNodeIds":{"triangle-sums-and-linear-pair":["cnm_115","cnm_062"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_115","cnm_062"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_110","cnm_059"],"id":"triangle-sums-and-straight-angle","label":"Use triangle sums and a straight angle","methodDirectNodeIds":{"triangle-sums-and-straight-angle":["cnm_115","cnm_062"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_115","cnm_062","cnm_110","cnm_059"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E. 70°","reason":"Let alpha=angle PAB and beta=angle PBA, so alpha+beta=140°. Tangency gives angle BAO=(180°-alpha)/2 and angle ABO=(180°-beta)/2. Hence angle AOB=(alpha+beta)/2=70°.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1956_AMC_12_Q49","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_062"],"id":"1956_AMC_12_Q49_m1","label":"Use the exterior tangent-angle bisectors","methodDirectNodeIds":{"1956_AMC_12_Q49_m1":["cnm_263","cnm_115"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_263","cnm_115","cnm_062"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: (180-A)/2","reason":"The angles at B and C in triangle BDC are (180-B)/2 and (180-C)/2. Thus angle BDC=(B+C)/2=(180-A)/2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1962_AMC_12_Q07","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_062"],"id":"exterior-bisector-angle-sum","label":"Find the other two angles of triangle BDC","methodDirectNodeIds":{"exterior-bisector-angle-sum":["cnm_058","cnm_115"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_058","cnm_115","cnm_062"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: sqrt(166)","reason":"In triangle AOB, cos(AOB)=(8^2+4^2-6^2)/(2*8*4)=11/16. Therefore cos(AOD)=-11/16, giving AD^2=8^2+6^2-2*8*6*(-11/16)=166.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":["The length OC=3 is not needed for either listed route.","OC = 3 is unnecessary for either listed route."],"questionId":"1967_AMC_12_Q32","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"general-cosine-across-diagonal","label":"Recover the arbitrary cosine and use its supplementary value","methodDirectNodeIds":{"general-cosine-across-diagonal":["math:general-cosine-law-application","cnm_062","cnm_h064"]},"nodeIds":["math:general-cosine-law-application","cnm_062","cnm_h064"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: r=1","reason":"Writing the quadrilateral's interior angles as A,B,C,D gives S=(180-C)+(180-D)=360-C-D. Since A+B+C+D=360, S=A+B=S', so r=1.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1968_AMC_12_Q06","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"quadrilateral-and-supplements","label":"Use quadrilateral angles and supplements","methodDirectNodeIds":{"quadrilateral-and-supplements":["cnm_181","cnm_062"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_181","cnm_062"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_053","cnm_056","cnm_180","cnm_059"],"id":"quadrilateral-supplements","label":"Method 2: Use supplementary angles","methodDirectNodeIds":{"quadrilateral-supplements":["cnm_058","cnm_181","cnm_062"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_058","cnm_181","cnm_062","cnm_053","cnm_056","cnm_180","cnm_059"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 22","reason":"An interior angle is 180-360/n, integral exactly when n divides 360. Since 360=2³·3²·5, it has 4·3·2=24 positive divisors. Excluding 1 and 2 leaves 22.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1993_AMC_12_Q15","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"exterior-angle-formula","label":"Use the exterior-angle formula","methodDirectNodeIds":{"exterior-angle-formula":["cnm_182","cnm_062"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_182","cnm_062"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_180","cnm_0