"C (120°)","reason":"AB = AE = BE, so angle AEB is 60°. With C and D the outer intersections on line AB, CB and AD are diameters. Hence angles CEB and AED are each 90°, giving angle CED = 90° + 90° - 60° = 120°.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2016_AMC_8_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"isosceles-angle-chase","label":"Use the outer isosceles triangles","methodDirectNodeIds":{"isosceles-angle-chase":["cnm_137","cnm_062","cnm_115"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_137","cnm_062","cnm_115"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p188","cnm_056"],"id":"radius-triangles","label":"Use triangles formed by radii","methodDirectNodeIds":{"radius-triangles":["cnm_058","cnm_139","cnm_137","cnm_062","cnm_p124"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_058","cnm_139","cnm_137","cnm_062","cnm_p124","cnm_p188","cnm_056"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"130","reason":"∠AOC=∠BOD=100°/2=50°. Since ∠AOD and ∠AOC form a linear pair, ∠AOD=180°−50°=130°.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"cn-math","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_063_q4","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"check-point-order","label":"Check the possible point orders","methodDirectNodeIds":{"check-point-order":["cnm_053","cnm_063","cnm_062","cnm_058"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_053","cnm_063","cnm_062","cnm_058"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"90°","reason":"SSS gives △ABD≅△ACD, so ∠ADB=∠ADC. Since DB and DC are opposite rays, these equal angles sum to 180°, making each 90°.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"cn-math","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_126_q4","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"id":"sss-and-linear-pair","label":"SSS and equal supplements","nodeIds":["cnm_122","cnm_121","cnm_062"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_120"],"id":"solution:sss-and-linear-pair:df1b7662ed11","label":"SSS and equal supplementary angles","methodDirectNodeIds":{"sss-and-linear-pair":["cnm_122","cnm_121","cnm_062"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_122","cnm_121","cnm_062","cnm_120"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"30°","reason":"Angle ACB=60°, so angle ACD=120°. Since AC=CD, the other two angles of ACD are equal, giving angle D=(180°-120°)/2=30°.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"cn-math","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_139_q5","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_062","cnm_115"],"id":"m1","label":"Use the exterior angle and equal base angles","methodDirectNodeIds":{"m1":["cnm_139","cnm_137"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_139","cnm_137","cnm_062","cnm_115"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A: 360°","reason":"Consistently oriented exterior turns around a simple polygon make one full revolution, totaling 360° independently of the number of sides.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"cn-math","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":["For a concave polygon, the full-turn statement uses signed, consistently oriented exterior angles rather than arbitrary unsigned angles.","The exterior-angle argument here uses the usual convex-polygon convention. The wording does not state it; arbitrary unsigned outside angles of a concave polygon need not sum to 360 degrees."],"questionId":"cnm_182_q2","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_062"],"id":"cnm_182_q2_m1","label":"Subtract interior angles from straight angles","methodDirectNodeIds":{"cnm_182_q2_m1":["cnm_182","cnm_181","cnm_071"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_182","cnm_181","cnm_071","cnm_062"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"22.5°","reason":"Angle ACB = 45°, so angle ACE = 135°. Since AC = CE, the two base angles of triangle ACE are equal, each measuring (180° - 135°) / 2 = 22.5°.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"cn-math","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_191_q5","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_062","cnm_115"],"id":"current","label":"Use the isosceles triangle","methodDirectNodeIds":{"current":["cnm_191","cnm_137"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_191","cnm_137","cnm_062","cnm_115"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, S=2700° and P may or may not be regular","reason":"If e is an exterior angle, then 7.5e+e=180°, so e=360°/17. There are 17 vertices and S=15·180°=2700°. Equal angles do not force equal sides; a regular 17-gon works, and a small parallel shift of one side produces a nonregular equiangular example.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1960_AMC_12_Q27","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_062","cnm_069"],"id":"exterior-angle-and-regularity","label":"Find the angles and test regularity","methodDirectNodeIds":{"exterior-angle-and-regularity":["cnm_180","cnm_181","cnm_182"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_180","cnm_181","cnm_182","cnm_062","cnm_069"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E. For every 0°<θ<60°","reason":"Put r=AB=AD. The sine rule gives AC=2r cosθ and BC=r(4cos²θ-1). With C at coordinate 0 on line CB, the two circle intersections have coordinate sum 2AC cosθ=4r cos²θ. Since B has coordinate BC, E has coordinate r, so CE=r=AD. At θ=45°, E=B and the same equality holds.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1975_AMC_12_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_250","cnm_062"],"id":"construct-two-isosceles-triangles","label":"Construct two isosceles triangles","methodDirectNodeIds":{"construct-two-isosceles-triangles":["cnm_138","cnm_115","cnm_261"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_138","cnm_115","cnm_261","cnm_250","cnm_062"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 30°","reason":"Because C is the circle's center, ∠ACB = 60° is a central angle. D is on the opposite side of the circle from A, so ∠ADB subtends the 60° minor arc AB and equals 30°.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1956_AMC_12_Q10","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_139","cnm_250","cnm_062"],"id":"isosceles-triangle","label":"Method 1: Use an isosceles triangle","methodDirectNodeIds":{"isosceles-triangle":["cnm_058","cnm_137","cnm_115"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_058","cnm_137","cnm_115","cnm_139","cnm_250","cnm_062"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: a=(b+c)/2","reason":"Let the equal base angles be t. Triangle BDF gives b=t+a-60°, and triangle CEF gives c=a+60°-t. Adding yields b+c=2a.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1960_AMC_12_Q38","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_056","cnm_058"],"id":"angle-sums","label":"Compare the two base triangles","methodDirectNodeIds":{"angle-sums":["cnm_115","cnm_137","cnm_139","cnm_062"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_115","cnm_137","cnm_139","cnm_062","cnm_056","cnm_058"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, 180/7 degrees = 25 5/7 degrees","reason":"Let angle B=θ and each base angle be α. From AC=AP, angle CAP=θ. From BQ=PQ, angle AQP=2θ; AP=PQ then gives angle PAQ=2θ. Thus α=3θ and 2α+θ=180°, yielding θ=180°/7.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1961_AMC_12_Q25","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_062","cnm_058"],"id":"isosceles-angle-chase-and-endpoints","label":"Follow the equal-side angle relations","methodDirectNodeIds":{"isosceles-angle-chase-and-endpoints":["cnm_137","cnm_115","cnm_138","cnm_139"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_137","cnm_115","cnm_138","cnm_139","cnm_062","cnm_058"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: 120°","reason":"For v_n = A_(n+1) - A_n, the midpoint rule gives v_(n+2) = -v_(n+1) - v_n/2, hence v_(n+4) = -v_n/4. Therefore triples (A43,A44,A45) and (A3,A4,A5) are similar in that order. Since A4A5 is parallel to A1A3, ∠A4A5A3 = 180° - 60° = 120°.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1978_AMC_12_Q28","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_055","cnm_139","cnm_062"],"id":"four-step-midsegment-similarity","label":"Show that triangle shapes repeat every four steps","methodDirectNodeIds":{"four-step-midsegment-similarity":["cnm_185","cnm_271","cnm_272"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_185","cnm_271","cnm_272","cnm_055","cnm_139","cnm_062"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 15°","reason":"Let x=∠BAO. Since AB=OB, ∠AOB=x and ∠ABO=180°−2x. Collinearity of A,B,E gives ∠OBE=2x, so ∠BEO=2x. Also ∠AOE=180°−45°=135°. Triangle AOE therefore gives x+2x+135°=180°, hence x=15°.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1979_AMC_12_Q12","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p188","cnm_062","cnm_115","cnm_p145"],"id":"1979_AMC_12_Q12_m1","label":"Use two isosceles triangles","methodDirectNodeIds":{"1979_AMC_12_Q12_m1":["cnm_058","cnm_137"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_058","cnm_137","cnm_p188","cnm_062","cnm_115","cnm_p145"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: 90°","reason":"Let α = ∠BAE = ∠ACD. Since ∠AEB = 120°, ∠ABC = 60° - α, while ∠ACB = 60° + α. Hence ∠BAC = 60°. Writing AC = s and AB = 2s, the cosine law gives BC² = 3s². Therefore AC² + BC² = AB², so ∠ACB = 90°.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2010_AMC_10A_Q14","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_062","cnm_058"],"id":"midpoint-and-isosceles-angles","label":"Method 1: A midpoint construction","methodDirectNodeIds":{"midpoint-and-isosceles-angles":["cnm_115","cnm_139","cnm_055","cnm_137"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_115","cnm_139","cnm_055","cnm_137","cnm_062","cnm_058"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_062","cnm_058","cnm_055"],"id":"altitude-and-pythagorean-converse","label":"Method 2: An altitude and side squares","methodDirectNodeIds":{"altitude-and-pythagorean-converse":["cnm_115","cnm_139","cnm_112","cnm_140","cnm_174","cnm_177"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_115","cnm_139","cnm_112","cnm_140","cnm_174","cnm_177","cnm_062","cnm_058","cnm_055"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B (25°)","reason":"An inscribed angle is half the central angle subtending the same arc, so ∠CAB=50°/2=25°.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2010_AMC_10B_Q06","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_250","cnm_058"],"id":"equal-radii","label":"Use the equal radii","methodDirectNodeIds":{"equal-radii":["cnm_062","cnm_137","cnm_115"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_062","cnm_137","cnm_115","cnm_250","cnm_058"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"adjudication":"Native mathematical/wording adjudication of the exact displayed task. Proposal for root inspection; raw targeted review is not blind diverse corroboration.","derivedAnswer":"85 degrees for opposite ray pairs;275/3 degrees for another configuration satisfying the displayed givens.","reason":"Saying that full linesAB andCD intersect atO does not state thatO lies betweenA,B and also betweenC,D. With both pairs opposite, the named angles are vertical and x=25 gives85 degrees. Keep A=(1,0),B=(-1,0),O=(0,0), but put C on a ray at275/3 degrees andD=2C on the same ray. Then angleAOC=275/3 and angleBOD=265/3, satisfying the two given expressions with x=80/3. Both full lines still intersect atO. No displayed figure supplies the missing point order. The topic heading identifies intended vertical-angle content but does not impose the missing ray incidence.","recordedAnswer":"85","status":"confirmed_ambiguous"},"assessed":false,"bank":"cn-math","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":["confirmed_answer_ambiguity","missing_point_order_or_ray_incidence"],"questionId":"cnm_063_q3","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_042","cnm_029"],"id":"analyze-ray-placements","label":"Consider the possible ray placements","methodDirectNodeIds":{"analyze-ray-placements":["cnm_053","cnm_063","cnm_062","cnm_058"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_053","cnm_063","cnm_062","cnm_058","cnm_042","cnm_029"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C","reason":"Equal compass radii give CA=CB and DA=DB. Thus C and D both lie on the perpendicular bisector of AB, and their joining line is that bisector.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"cn-math","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_134_q1","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"id":"sss-at-midpoint","label":"SSS at the midpoint","nodeIds":["cnm_055","cnm_122","cnm_121","cnm_062","cnm_064"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_055","cnm_054","cnm_p188"],"id":"solution:sss-at-midpoint:4e8867d8106d","label":"Congruent triangles at the midpoint","methodDirectNodeIds":{"sss-at-midpoint":["cnm_134","cnm_122","cnm_121","cnm_062","cnm_064"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_134","cnm_122","cnm_121","cnm_062","cnm_064","cnm_055","cnm_054","cnm_p188"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"8","reason":"The interior sum is 3·360°=1080°. Thus (n-2)·180°=1080°, giving n=8.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"cn-math","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":["The exterior-angle argument here uses the usual convex-polygon convention. The wording does not state it; arbitrary unsigned outside angles of a concave polygon need not sum to 360 degrees."],"questionId":"cnm_182_q4","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_040","cnm_058","cnm_062"],"id":"m1","label":"Angle sums with the convention stated","methodDirectNodeIds":{"m1":["cnm_181","cnm_182","cnm_071"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_181","cnm_182","cnm_071","cnm_040","cnm_058","cnm_062"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, CD = OD = BD","reason":"Write A = 2α, B = 2β, C = 2γ. Inscribed angles give ∠CBD = ∠BCD = α, so BD = CD. Also ∠DBO = α+β, while ∠BOD = 180°-∠BOA = 90°-γ = α+β. Thus BD = OD as well.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1966_AMC_12_Q31","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_114","cnm_115","cnm_062","cnm_250"],"id":"incenter-and-inscribed-angle-chase","label":"Find two isosceles triangles","methodDirectNodeIds":{"incenter-and-inscribed-angle-chase":["cnm_264","cnm_253","cnm_138"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_264","cnm_253","cnm_138","cnm_114","cnm_115","cnm_062","cnm_250"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A. 2","reason":"The cosine rule gives BC²=3²+6²−2·3·6 cos120°=63. The bisector theorem gives CD:DB=1:2, so CD·DB=14. Stewart's relation then gives AD²=3·6−14=4, hence AD=2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1983_AMC_12_Q19","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_058","cnm_062","cnm_069"],"id":"equilateral-extension-and-similarity","label":"Build an equilateral triangle","methodDirectNodeIds":{"equilateral-extension-and-similarity":["cnm_139","cnm_068","cnm_271","cnm_272"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_139","cnm_068","cnm_271","cnm_272","cnm_058","cnm_062","cnm_069"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 5","reason":"Arc CD=60° gives ∠CAO=30°. Since OA=OC, ∠OCB=30°. Also ∠OBC=180°−60°=120°, so ∠BOC=30°. Thus BC=BO=5.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1985_AMC_12_Q22","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_250","cnm_056"],"id":"circle-angle-chase","label":"Find equal angles in triangle BOC","methodDirectNodeIds":{"circle-angle-chase":["cnm_253","cnm_137","cnm_138","cnm_062","cnm_115"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_253","cnm_137","cnm_138","cnm_062","cnm_115","cnm_250","cnm_056"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: a+b","reason":"Let X be on AB with DX parallel to BC. Parallelogram XBCD gives XB=CD=b. The parallel-line angle relations and angle D=2 angle B give angle ADX=angle AXD=angle B, so AX=AD=a. Thus AB=a+b.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1970_AMC_12_Q30","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_055","cnm_058","cnm_062","cnm_066","cnm_067"],"id":"extend-to-isosceles-triangles","label":"Extend the legs to form two isosceles triangles","methodDirectNodeIds":{"extend-to-isosceles-triangles":["cnm_069","cnm_115","cnm_138"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_069","cnm_115","cnm_138","cnm_055","cnm_058","cnm_062","cnm_066","cnm_067"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B: 15°","reason":"Let t be half the measure of each equal arc and u = ∠ACD. The full circle gives 3t + u = 180°. Triangle ACE gives t - u = 40°. Solving yields u = 15°.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1977_AMC_12_Q09","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_250","cnm_058","cnm_110","cnm_042"],"id":"inscribed-angles-and-triangle","label":"Relate the arcs to triangle ACE","methodDirectNodeIds":{"inscribed-angles-and-triangle":["cnm_253","cnm_062","cnm_115","cnm_089"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_253","cnm_062","cnm_115","cnm_089","cnm_250","cnm_058","cnm_110","cnm_042"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A. 5","reason":"Extend BP beyond P to Q with PQ=3. Then DB=1 and DQ=5. Isosceles triangle APQ gives ∠AQD=∠BCD, and vertical angles give triangle DAQ similar to triangle DBC. Thus AD·CD=DB·DQ=5.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1997_AMC_12_Q26","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_053","cnm_055","cnm_062","cnm_063","cnm_117","cnm_268"],"id":"solution:extension-and-similarity:653647296e1f","label":"Extend a segment to create similar triangles","methodDirectNodeIds":{"extension-and-similarity":["cnm_137","cnm_115","cnm_271","cnm_272"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_137","cnm_115","cnm_271","cnm_272","cnm_053","cnm_055","cnm_062","cnm_063","cnm_117","cnm_268"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_053","cnm_055","cnm_062","cnm_117","cnm_250"],"id":"auxiliary-circle","label":"Recognize an intersecting-chord product","methodDirectNodeIds":{"auxiliary-circle":["cnm_137","cnm_115","math:power-of-a-point"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_137","cnm_115","math:power-of-a-point","cnm_053","cnm_055","cnm_062","cnm_117","cnm_250"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":8,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B: 35","reason":"Choose D on AB with ∠ACD=∠A. Angle sums give AD=CD and BD=BC=27, hence AD=CD=21. Stewart's theorem gives 27b²+27²×21=48(21²+21×27), so b²=1225 and b=35.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1985_AMC_12_Q28","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_115","cnm_062","cnm_055","cnm_148","cnm_074","cnm_040"],"id":"isosceles-construction-pythagoras","label":"Split the triple angle into isosceles triangles","methodDirectNodeIds":{"isosceles-construction-pythagoras":["cnm_138","cnm_174","cnm_082"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_138","cnm_174","cnm_082","cnm_115","cnm_062","cnm_055","cnm_148","cnm_074","cnm_040"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":8,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, 75°","reason":"Let F be the perpendicular foot from D to CM. Then DF = AD = 3 and DC = 6, giving angle DCM = 30°. Parallel sides give angle CDM = angle AMD = angle CMD, so twice the requested angle plus 30° equals 180°.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2011_AMC_10B_Q18","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p077","cnm_183","cnm_062"],"id":"equal-angles-and-equilateral-reflection","label":"Use equal angles, then construct an equilateral triangle","methodDirectNodeIds":{"equal-angles-and-equilateral-reflection":["cnm_058","cnm_069","cnm_138","cnm_132","cnm_139","cnm_137"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_058","cnm_069","cnm_138","cnm_132","cnm_139","cnm_137","cnm_p077","cnm_183","cnm_062"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":9,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 75°","reason":"Set B=(0,0), D=(1,0), and C=(3,0). Since ∠ABC=45°, write A=(t,t). The 60° direction of DA gives t=sqrt(3)(t-1), so t=(3+sqrt(3))/2. Therefore tan(∠ACB)=t/(3-t)=2+sqrt(3)=tan(75°). The angle is acute, hence it is 75°.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2001_AMC_12_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_055","cnm_058","cnm_062","cnm_121"],"id":"equilateral-and-isosceles-construction","label":"Create matching isosceles triangles","methodDirectNodeIds":{"equilateral-and-isosceles-construction":["cnm_130","cnm_139","cnm_123","cnm_137","cnm_138","cnm_115"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_130","cnm_139","cnm_123","cnm_137","cnm_138","cnm_115","cnm_055","cnm_058","cnm_062","cnm_121"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":10,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: 2x+1","reason":"Extend BA beyond A to D with AD=AC=x. The equal arcs give ∠MAC=∠MAD, so SAS yields MD=MC=MB. Thus MP bisects DB, and PB=PD=AP+AD=(x+1)+x=2x+1.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1972_AMC_12_Q26","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_250","cnm_055","cnm_120","cnm_064","cnm_062"],"id":"extend-chord","label":"Extend the chord to build an isosceles triangle","methodDirectNodeIds":{"extend-chord":["cnm_252","cnm_253","cnm_130","cnm_123","cnm_121","cnm_137"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_252","cnm_253","cnm_130","cnm_123","cnm_121","cnm_137","cnm_250","cnm_055","cnm_120","cnm_064","cnm_062"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":11,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C (7√3 - 12)","reason":"The minor arcs AB and AC are each 150°, and AD is 120°. Equality DG = AB, together with BG > GA, forces arc AG = 30°, making GC a diameter. Right-triangle angle relations give AF/AB = tan15° = 2 - √3 and AE/AC = √3tan15° = 2√3 - 3. Their product is 7√3 - 12.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1981_AMC_12_Q27","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_250","cnm_115","cnm_062","cnm_173"],"id":"arcs-and-special-right-triangles","label":"Find the arcs, then use special triangles","methodDirectNodeIds":{"arcs-and-special-right-triangles":["cnm_252","cnm_253","cnm_139","cnm_138","cnm_137","cnm_140","cnm_174","cnm_p148"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_252","cnm_253","cnm_139","cnm_138","cnm_137","cnm_140","cnm_174","cnm_p148","cnm_250","cnm_115","cnm_062","cnm_173"]}]}],"layer":"ailleo","meaning":"Reviewed content-bound concept practice; no claim of complete source-standard coverage or learner mastery.","nextOffset":null,"nodeId":"cnm_062","status":"mapped","total":53}
